Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "material-culture": materialArticle({
    slug:"material-culture",title:"Material culture and civilian industry",eyebrow:"Comparative social and industrial history · 1947–1985",infoboxKicker:"Postwar civilian systems",
    lead:"Material culture after the 1947 armistice develops through conversion and improvisation, applied systems, and finally platforms and networks. The period does not replace the old world with a clean technological future: wartime machines, traditional homes, paper institutions, mass appliances, nuclear grids, CRTs, and incompatible computers remain layered together.",
    canon:"The three-stage development, bloc characteristics, major civilian technologies, and 1985 household limits are established. Exact national adoption rates, several product standards, and disputed corporate structures remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"transformations",title:"Three overlapping transformations",html:"<ol><li><strong>Conversion and improvisation, 1947–1955:</strong> combines, workshops, surplus vehicles, and reconstruction agencies keep society moving.</li><li><strong>Applied systems, 1955–1970:</strong> reliability, transistors, automation, refrigeration, nuclear engineering, logistics, jets, and space procurement enter ordinary production.</li><li><strong>Platforms and networks, 1970–1985:</strong> compact electronics, machine-readable commerce, home video, personal computing, and bloc networks connect products to systems.</li></ol>"},
      {id:"inheritance",title:"The industrial inheritance",html:"<p>German and Japanese firms are not dismantled by occupation. Their combines, zaibatsu, aircraft works, shipyards, patents, machine tools, supplier networks, and political ties survive. Italy preserves IRI. Britain reconstructs through dispersed Commonwealth production. America retains a private consumer industry shaped by prolonged depression rather than total-war mobilization.</p><p>Foreign-owned firms often remain legally intact while dividends, boards, procurement, and reinvestment are politically constrained.</p>"},
      {id:"objects",title:"Objects as political promises",html:"<p>Germany's people's car, Italy's scooter and designed household object, Japan's powered bicycle and compact radio, Britain's reconstructed housing and Land-Rover, and America's automobile, television, refrigerator, and appliance each symbolize a governing promise.</p><p>None functions alone: electricity, roads, broadcasting, credit, service, spare parts, and standards determine whether the object becomes ordinary.</p>"},
      {id:"systems",title:"Applied systems",html:"<p>The space and nuclear races popularize complex systems without directly filling homes with space hardware. Supplier inspection, serialized parts, seals, lubricants, controls, cold storage, container handling, weather services, and maintenance schedules produce the greatest everyday changes.</p><p>Technical modernity is therefore visible in reliable service as much as in spectacular inventions.</p>"},
      {id:"bloc",title:"Bloc character",html:"<p>Germany emphasizes coordinated continental systems and certification. Japan develops compact useful machines and Asia-first brands. Britain prizes practical reliability at distance. America joins private abundance to federal research. The Latin bloc combines design with multinational integration at moderate scale.</p><p>These are tendencies rather than exclusive inventions. Firms, languages, components, and methods cross borders under licenses and gateways.</p>"},
      {id:"american-firms",title:"American industrial institutions",html:"<p><a href='ibm.html'>IBM</a> anchors institutional computation and office systems. <a href='general-electric.html'>General Electric</a> and <a href='westinghouse.html'>Westinghouse</a> span power, appliances, industrial control, and nuclear engineering. <a href='rca.html'>RCA</a> integrates broadcasting, recording, television, and communications. <a href='motorola.html'>Motorola</a> specializes in mobile radio, semiconductors, and public-safety electronics.</p><p><a href='zenith-electronics.html'>Zenith</a> sustains independent receiver engineering. <a href='xerox.html'>Xerox</a> develops office reproduction and networked-work research. <a href='polaroid.html'>Polaroid</a> specializes in instant and scientific imaging. <a href='corning-glass-works.html'>Corning</a> supplies technical glass and fiber. <a href='fairchild-semiconductor.html'>Fairchild</a> and <a href='texas-instruments.html'>Texas Instruments</a> develop integrated circuits.</p><p><a href='ncr.html'>NCR</a>, <a href='remington-rand.html'>Remington Rand</a>, and <a href='burroughs-corporation.html'>Burroughs</a> turn registers, typewriters, files, and accounting machines into electronic transaction systems. <a href='philco.html'>Philco</a> joins radio and appliances to semiconductor and computer work. <a href='eastman-kodak.html'>Eastman Kodak</a> supplies the chemical and optical infrastructure of mass imaging. The <a href='bell-system.html'>Bell System</a> joins universal telephony to long-horizon research, while <a href='western-union.html'>Western Union</a> connects written messaging and money transfer to microwave, satellite, and business-data networks.</p><p><a href='dupont.html'>DuPont</a>, <a href='dow-chemical.html'>Dow Chemical</a>, <a href='union-carbide.html'>Union Carbide</a>, and <a href='monsanto.html'>Monsanto</a> supply fibers, films, polymers, insulation, gases, agricultural compounds, coatings, and process materials. Their products enter homes and machines through thousands of downstream manufacturers. Together these institutions connect private consumer abundance to federal research and mission procurement while remaining distinct corporations.</p>"},
      {id:"british-firms",title:"British materials, services, and commercial systems",html:"<p><a href='imperial-chemical-industries.html'>Imperial Chemical Industries</a>, <a href='dunlop.html'>Dunlop</a>, <a href='courtaulds.html'>Courtaulds</a>, and <a href='pilkington.html'>Pilkington</a> supply the chemicals, polymers, rubber goods, tyres, fibres, textiles, and glass behind reconstruction. Their laboratories connect civilian housing and clothing to aviation, nuclear power, transport, and Commonwealth supply.</p><p><a href='boots.html'>Boots</a> turns pharmacy, pharmaceutical research, health goods, and national distribution into an everyday service institution. <a href='j-lyons.html'>J. Lyons and Co.</a> links catering and food logistics to the LEO commercial-computing tradition, showing how administrative systems can emerge from shops, bakeries, and delivery routes rather than military laboratories alone.</p>"},
      {id:"british-shipping",title:"British shipping institutions",html:"<p><a href='p-and-o.html'>P&amp;O</a> and the <a href='british-india-steam-navigation-company.html'>British India Steam Navigation Company</a> connect Britain to the Indian Ocean, Asian waters, and Australasia. <a href='union-castle-line.html'>Union-Castle</a> operates the South African mail and passenger-cargo axis. <a href='elder-dempster-lines.html'>Elder Dempster</a> and the <a href='blue-funnel-line.html'>Blue Funnel Line</a> organize West African and Asian cargo services from Liverpool, while <a href='cunard-white-star.html'>Cunard–White Star</a> carries North Atlantic passengers, mail, cargo, and prestige.</p><p>Fleet finance, mail contracts, conference agreements, insurance, port agencies, container partnerships, and emergency charters bind nominally private companies to the Siege Commonwealth. Their ships carry household food and manufactured exports in peace and personnel, stores, hospitals, and headquarters in war.</p>"},
      {id:"british-shipbuilders",title:"British shipbuilding institutions",html:"<p><a href='vickers-armstrongs.html'>Vickers-Armstrongs</a>, <a href='john-brown-and-company.html'>John Brown &amp; Company</a>, <a href='cammell-laird.html'>Cammell Laird</a>, <a href='swan-hunter.html'>Swan Hunter</a>, and <a href='harland-and-wolff.html'>Harland &amp; Wolff</a> preserve distinct concentrations of naval architecture, marine machinery, steelwork, submarine construction, large-hull assembly, repair, and fitting out.</p><p>Public finance and private operation bind their yards to merchant-fleet renewal, arsenal continuity, Dominion production, and the Cape-route system. Their products are strategic infrastructure, workplaces, and the physical carriers of civilian food, fuel, migration, and trade.</p>"},
      {id:"british-motoring",title:"British motor institutions",html:"<p>The <a href='british-motor-group.html'>British Motor Group</a> supplies compact and medium passenger cars; <a href='leyland-motors.html'>Leyland Motors</a> specializes in trucks and buses; and the <a href='rover-company.html'>Rover Company</a> combines professional saloons with the <a href='land-rover.html'>Land-Rover</a> utility system. <a href='jaguar-cars.html'>Jaguar</a>, <a href='lotus-cars.html'>Lotus</a>, and the <a href='cooper-car-company.html'>Cooper Car Company</a> preserve luxury, sports-car, chassis, competition, and contract-engineering niches.</p><p>The <a href='motor-industry-of-britain.html'>national industry</a> remains broad but divided. Commonwealth fleets and specialist exports are stronger than mass-car quality and plant rationalization.</p>"},
      {id:"british-electrical",title:"British electrical and electronic institutions",html:"<p><a href='ferranti.html'>Ferranti</a>, the British <a href='general-electric-company-britain.html'>General Electric Company</a>, and <a href='english-electric.html'>English Electric</a> connect grid equipment, industrial machinery, radar, computing, atomic power, aerospace, and control. <a href='pye.html'>Pye</a> and <a href='emi.html'>EMI</a> connect radio, television, recording, mobile communications, instruments, and consumer electronics.</p><p>The <a href='electrical-and-electronic-industry-of-britain.html'>national electrical and electronic industry</a> retains several overlapping firms. <a href='international-computers-limited.html'>ICL</a> consolidates much general-purpose commercial computing while specialized and real-time lines remain distributed.</p>"},
      {id:"platform",title:"Platform society",html:"<p>After 1975, purchasers increasingly choose an ecosystem of software, support, peripherals, media, character handling, finance, and political access rather than one isolated device. Similar dynamics appear in video formats, game consoles, electronic records, and telecommunications.</p><p>Compatibility becomes a form of institutional allegiance.</p>"},
      {id:"inequality",title:"Uneven modernity",html:"<p>A metropolitan household may own color television and video while a nearby subject district depends on repaired machinery and hand records. The frontier follows ports, power grids, bases, hospitals, laboratories, favored schools, and supply chains rather than national borders.</p><p>The same system can produce technical competence, consumer prosperity, surveillance, and colonial extraction.</p>"},
      {id:"look",title:"The ordinary look of 1985",html:"<p>A prosperous room combines traditional furniture, religious or dynastic images, a large CRT, video recorder, stereo, plastic telephone, personal computer, and paper files. Streets combine interwar masonry, neoclassical monuments, technical-modern additions, scooters, buses, neon, and electronic signs.</p><p>The future arrives as accumulated layers, not aesthetic replacement.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"everyday-life.html",kicker:"Comparative society",label:"Everyday Life by Bloc"},{href:"platform-society.html",kicker:"Digital transition",label:"Consumer Computing and Platform Society"}],facts:[["Reference period","1947–1985"],["First phase","Conversion and improvisation"],["Second phase","Applied systems"],["Third phase","Platforms and networks"],["Defining labor figure","Certified maintenance technician"],["1985 display technology","Cathode-ray tube"],["Universal consumer network","Absent"],["Central pattern","Layered and unequal modernization"]]
  }),

  "industrial-conversion-1947": materialArticle({
    slug:"industrial-conversion-1947",title:"Industrial conversion after 1947",eyebrow:"Postwar economic transition · 1947–1955",infoboxKicker:"Civilian conversion era",
    lead:"Industrial conversion after the 1947 armistice is the uneven redirection of wartime plants, transport, machine tools, labor, and procurement toward reconstruction and civilian production. Unlike the familiar postwar settlement, victorious German and Japanese combines are neither dismantled nor purged from economic command.",
    canon:"The preservation of major bloc industries, incomplete 1947 conversion, foreign-ownership problem, small-firm dependence, and early consumer symbols are established. Individual company settlements may remain disputed.",
    sections:[
      {id:"armistice",title:"The unfinished armistice economy",html:"<p>No major bloc completes conversion by the end of 1947. Military orders fall unevenly, armies retain equipment, shipping remains scarce, cities require repair, and food and fuel systems still operate under emergency rules.</p><p>Governments therefore convert capacity while continuing security and imperial procurement.</p>"},
      {id:"germany",title:"German industrial continuity",html:"<p><a href='ig-farben.html'>IG Farben</a>, the <a href='reichswerke-hermann-goering.html'>Reichswerke</a>, automobile groups, electrical combines, optics houses, and aerospace concerns retain their staff, suppliers, patents, and political leverage. Civilian production grows through controlled allocations rather than a competitive reset.</p><p>Eastern reconstruction and settler supply remain inseparable from coercive imperial extraction.</p>"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese imperial conversion",html:"<p>Zaibatsu, Nakajima capacity, shipyards, electrical firms, optics makers, and precision workshops survive victory. Their first civilian work must still support a maritime empire: ships, ports, radios, light vehicles, refrigeration, and administrative equipment.</p>"},
      {id:"west",title:"Britain, America, and the Latin bloc",html:"<p>Britain uses mixed state, private, and Commonwealth reconstruction industries. America expands from a depressed private consumer base rather than demobilizing a complete wartime production state. Italy preserves IRI and major mobility, aviation, office-machine, energy, and shipbuilding concerns.</p>"},
      {id:"ownership",title:"Blocked and constrained ownership",html:"<p>Opel, Ford-Werke, Dehomag, and British or Dutch properties in Japanese Asia can remain legally foreign-owned while local states block dividends, compel reinvestment, control boards, and direct procurement.</p><p>Legal title and practical command become separate questions that recur during later commercial détente.</p>"},
      {id:"conversion",title:"From weapons to reconstruction",html:"<p>Truck plants produce haulage vehicles; aviation shops produce engines, tools, and transport components; electrical firms repair grids and radios; chemical plants supply fertilizers, insulation, tires, paints, and plastics. Conversion works best where a civilian distributor and maintenance network already exists.</p>"},
      {id:"symbols",title:"Consumer symbols",html:"<p>The people's car, scooter, Land-Rover, powered bicycle, refrigerator, and television turn conversion into a public story. They promise that victory, survival, or neutrality can be translated into ordinary comfort.</p><p>Shortages and waiting lists often make the promise more visible than the object itself.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>Conversion preserves industrial concentration and technical skill while entrenching firms already close to the state. New companies nevertheless emerge beneath them through repair, small engines, recording equipment, office machinery, refrigeration, and local distribution.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"postwar-repair-economy.html",kicker:"Local foundation",label:"Postwar Repair Economy"},{href:"surplus-motorization.html",kicker:"Vehicle conversion",label:"Surplus Motorization"},{href:"political-economy.html",kicker:"Bloc structures",label:"Political Economy by Bloc"}],facts:[["Beginning","1947 armistice"],["Initial condition","Conversion incomplete"],["German settlement","Major combines preserved"],["Japanese settlement","Zaibatsu preserved"],["British model","Mixed Commonwealth reconstruction"],["American condition","Depression-shaped private industry"],["Ownership problem","Legal title versus political control"],["Principal outcome","Continuity with selective civilian redirection"]]
  }),

  "postwar-repair-economy": materialArticle({
    slug:"postwar-repair-economy",title:"Postwar repair economy",eyebrow:"Small firms, maintenance, and reconstruction · 1947–1960",infoboxKicker:"Civilian small-firm economy",
    lead:"The postwar repair economy is the network of garages, remanufacturers, dealers, builders, equipment shops, cold stores, radio technicians, and transport firms that keeps mismatched wartime and civilian machinery operating. Before the prestige engineer becomes the emblem of modernity, the practical mechanic is its indispensable social figure.",
    canon:"The small-firm layer, principal trades, spare-parts and credit role, surplus dependence, and transition toward standardized service are established. Individual fictional firms remain largely unnamed.",
    sections:[
      {id:"need",title:"Why repair dominates",html:"<p>New production is insufficient, capital scarce, transport uneven, and imported components politically unreliable. Farms, municipalities, shops, armies, and families therefore extend the life of whatever machines they already possess.</p><p>A working repaired device is more valuable than a theoretically superior product with no local parts.</p>"},
      {id:"trades",title:"The small-firm layer",html:"<p>Repair garages, village mechanics, engine and tire remanufacturers, electrical-motor shops, pump and machine-tool rebuilders, builders, refrigeration contractors, radio technicians, and office-equipment services form the ordinary industrial base.</p><p>Regional bus, haulage, and dealer firms join technical work to transport and credit.</p>"},
      {id:"dealer",title:"Dealer, creditor, and parts broker",html:"<p>A dealer often sells used equipment, extends credit, locates scarce spares, arranges fuel, and recommends a mechanic. These functions matter more than the formal brand network in remote or poor regions.</p><p>Customer loyalty attaches to the person who can keep a machine working through several incompatible generations.</p>"},
      {id:"remanufacture",title:"Remanufacturing",html:"<p>Engines, generators, tires, motors, pumps, batteries, and machine tools are dismantled, measured, reconditioned, and combined from salvage. Replacement components may be locally cast or machined when original supply has disappeared.</p><p>Quality varies, but the best shops accumulate practical knowledge later absorbed by larger firms.</p>"},
      {id:"new-firms",title:"Incubation of new firms",html:"<p>Several later industrial names begin in unglamorous repair and compact equipment. <a href='honda-motor-company.html'>Honda</a> grows through powered bicycles and small engines. <a href='tokyo-tsushin-kogyo.html'>Tokyo Tsushin Kogyo</a> grows through recording and small electronics. Technical offices and reconstruction agencies also become permanent firms.</p>"},
      {id:"colonies",title:"Colonial and frontier persistence",html:"<p>Standardized new vehicles replace surplus first in metropolitan markets. Farms, colonies, eastern territories, remote islands, and municipal works continue to depend on mixed fleets and local improvisation well into the 1960s.</p><p>Repair skill can therefore mark both resilience and exclusion from newer infrastructure.</p>"},
      {id:"credentials",title:"From mechanic to technician",html:"<p>As electronics, automatic controls, refrigeration, and instruments spread, informal skill is supplemented by manuals, test equipment, manufacturer courses, and portable certificates. The repair economy becomes one root of the later <a href='technician-class.html'>technician class</a>.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>The repair economy prevents reconstruction from being wholly controlled by great combines. It creates proprietors, local credit, technical mobility, and gray markets while also making safety and quality difficult to regulate.</p><p>Even in 1985, mechanically legible vehicles and serviceable appliances preserve this culture beneath electronic platforms.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"technician-class.html",kicker:"Later occupation",label:"Certified Technician Class"},{href:"surplus-motorization.html",kicker:"Principal machinery",label:"Surplus Motorization"}],facts:[["High period","1947–1955"],["Principal actor","Practical mechanic"],["Core businesses","Repair, remanufacture, haulage, refrigeration"],["Dealer functions","Sales, credit, parts brokerage"],["Primary constraint","Fuel, tires, batteries, and spares"],["Frontier persistence","Into and beyond the 1960s"],["Later transformation","Certified technical service"]]
  }),

  "surplus-motorization": materialArticle({
    slug:"surplus-motorization",title:"Surplus motorization",eyebrow:"Civilian vehicle conversion · 1947–1955",infoboxKicker:"Postwar transport phenomenon",
    lead:"Surplus motorization is the civilian reuse of wartime trucks, utility cars, motorcycles, ambulances, trailers, workshops, generators, cranes, tractors, and engineering equipment after 1947. It accelerates reconstruction and rural transport while creating a looming replacement crisis once worn fleets exhaust their parts.",
    canon:"The principal vehicle classes, German and British release, Japanese retention, eastern captured fleet, four-stage chronology, and practical constraints are established.",
    sections:[
      {id:"release",title:"Release of military vehicles",html:"<p>Germany and Britain release large stocks of soft-skinned vehicles into agriculture, municipal work, reconstruction, approved private haulage, and colonial service. Japan releases a smaller share at home because its maritime empire continues to absorb military and logistical vehicles.</p>"},
      {id:"useful",title:"The useful inheritance",html:"<p>Trucks, utility cars, motorcycles, sidecars, ambulances, trailers, mobile workshops, generators, compressors, cranes, fuel bowsers, tractors, and engineering machines matter far more than tanks.</p><p>Tracked and armored vehicles survive mainly as recovery machines, forestry tractors, construction equipment, security assets, or scrap.</p>"},
      {id:"east",title:"Eastern and colonial fleets",html:"<p>The Reichskommissariats combine German releases with captured Soviet vehicles and machinery. Colonial administrations and firms use whatever standardized or surplus fleet their supply line can support.</p><p>A truck's political origin may be obscured by several repaints, engines, and locally made bodies.</p>"},
      {id:"constraints",title:"Fuel, tires, batteries, and parts",html:"<p>The number of chassis does not determine usable transport. Tires rot, batteries fail, fuels differ, engines wear, and undocumented conversions complicate maintenance.</p><p>Garages and remanufacturers determine how much of the paper fleet can actually move.</p>"},
      {id:"rhythm",title:"Chronology",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Character</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1947–1949</td><td>Release, salvage, improvised conversion, mixed paint</td></tr><tr><td>1950–1952</td><td>Maximum civilian utility and expanding repair networks</td></tr><tr><td>1953–1956</td><td>Replacement crisis as worn fleets become uneconomic</td></tr><tr><td>After 1955</td><td>New standard vehicles dominate metropolitan purchases; surplus persists on frontiers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"conversion",title:"Civilian conversion",html:"<p>Military bodies are replaced with buses, farm platforms, fire apparatus, delivery boxes, cranes, and workshops. New paint and company lettering establish civilian identity without erasing the original structure.</p><p>The period's defining image is a lorry whose worn civilian paint reveals a wartime marking beneath.</p>"},
      {id:"replacement",title:"The replacement crisis",html:"<p>By the mid-1950s, labor and parts spent preserving the worst vehicles exceed their transport value. Governments and firms must choose new standard families and train mechanics around them. Britain's most characteristic answer is the <a href='land-rover.html'>Land-Rover</a>, sold with parts, manuals, tools, training, and regional service.</p><p>This transition strengthens large manufacturers and bloc parts catalogues.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>Surplus motorization creates roads, routes, garages, drivers, small haulage companies, and expectations of mobile service before new civilian production can meet demand. It also leaves remote regions with aging mixed fleets long after metropolitan advertisements declare a new age.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"postwar-repair-economy.html",kicker:"Maintenance system",label:"Postwar Repair Economy"},{href:"logistics-technical-standards.html",kicker:"Replacement order",label:"Logistics and Technical Standards"}],facts:[["High period","1947–1955"],["Largest releasers","Germany and Britain"],["Japanese pattern","Greater imperial retention"],["Eastern supplement","Captured Soviet vehicles"],["Most useful type","Soft-skinned trucks and utilities"],["Primary ceiling","Consumables and spare parts"],["Replacement crisis","1953–1956"],["Visual emblem","Civilian paint over wartime marking"]]
  }),

  "applied-systems-takeoff": materialArticle({
    slug:"applied-systems-takeoff",title:"Applied-systems takeoff",eyebrow:"Civilian technological transformation · 1955–1970",infoboxKicker:"Industrial modernization period",
    lead:"The applied-systems takeoff is the period in which reliability engineering, transistors, automatic control, refrigeration, nuclear methods, modern logistics, jet certification, and space procurement begin changing ordinary products and services. Its signature is coordination rather than one miraculous invention.",
    canon:"The 1955–1970 periodization, Raumbote transition, principal technical spillovers, bloc baselines, and social consequences are established.",
    sections:[
      {id:"before",title:"Separate expert worlds",html:"<p>Before 1957, rocketry, radar, reactors, automatic control, jet propulsion, materials science, and electronic calculation are often funded and administered separately. Their suppliers overlap without being treated as one public system.</p>"},
      {id:"raumbote",title:"The Raumbote transition",html:"<p>Germany's Raumbote I proves that tracking, telemetry, control, materials, computation, communications, and launch operations can be coordinated nationally. Governments respond by purchasing laboratories, weather stations, teleprinters, gyroscopes, vacuum systems, machine tools, and technical-school places as well as rockets.</p>"},
      {id:"reliability",title:"Reliability and certification",html:"<p>Aircraft, reactors, submarines, factories, and launch vehicles show that complex systems fail through ordinary parts. Supplier inspection, traceability, fatigue testing, accident inquiry, and maintenance schedules become permanent institutions.</p><p>Appliances and vehicles gain better bearings, insulation, wiring, seals, and records.</p>"},
      {id:"electronics",title:"Transistors and control",html:"<p>Portable, low-power, shock-resistant transistors spread through radios, hearing aids, field telephones, medical monitors, test equipment, and compact controllers. Automation means relay sequences, gauges, transfer lines, telephone exchanges, and punched-tape machinery—not empty factories.</p>"},
      {id:"materials",title:"Materials, nuclear methods, and cold chains",html:"<p>Jet, rocket, and reactor work improves alloys, rubber, steels, ceramics, insulation, plastics, coatings, pumps, valves, instruments, radiography, and remote handling. Refrigerated warehouses, trucks, ships, milk systems, pharmaceuticals, and household refrigerators transform food and health.</p>"},
      {id:"logistics",title:"Logistics and standards",html:"<p>Pallets, forklifts, standardized crates, early containers, documents, and machine-readable records reduce handling and theft. Incompatible fittings, frequencies, plugs, and measurements bind smaller states to suppliers and training systems.</p>"},
      {id:"society",title:"Instrumented modernity",html:"<p>By the early 1960s ordinary citizens encounter computers through bills, payroll, reservations, and schedules; electronics through radios and control panels; space through weather and broadcasting; and nuclear technology through medicine and electricity.</p><p>Much of modernity is a service encountered indirectly.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Limits by 1970",html:"<p>Television and portable radio are mass media, but jets remain prestigious, automation still requires skilled labor, and homes remain conventionally furnished. Space-age styling appears in plastics, dashboards, switches, packaging, and advertising more often than in architecture.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"reliability-engineering.html",kicker:"Institutional method",label:"Reliability Engineering"},{href:"civilian-cold-chain.html",kicker:"Food and medicine",label:"Civilian Cold Chain"},{href:"instrumented-modernity.html",kicker:"Social experience",label:"Instrumented Modernity"}],facts:[["Period","1955–1970"],["Catalytic event","Raumbote I, 1957"],["Central method","Systems coordination"],["Key electronics","Transistors and automatic control"],["Major household symbol","Refrigerator"],["Major industrial institution","Reliability group"],["Logistics tools","Pallets, forklifts, early containers"],["Principal labor shift","Mechanic toward technician"]]
  }),

  "reliability-engineering": materialArticle({
    slug:"reliability-engineering",title:"Reliability engineering",eyebrow:"Quality, failure, and certification · from the 1950s",infoboxKicker:"Applied engineering discipline",
    lead:"Reliability engineering is the organized study and control of component failure across aircraft, reactors, submarines, launch vehicles, factories, vehicles, appliances, and communications. It transforms quality from final inspection into a documented relationship among design, supplier, environment, maintenance, and accident inquiry.",
    canon:"The principal methods, complex-system origins, Comet influence, civilian spillovers, and certification role are established. National standards and exact statistical procedures vary.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins in complex systems",html:"<p>High-speed aircraft, reactors, submarines, missiles, and launch vehicles reveal that ordinary seals, wires, fasteners, bearings, and inspection failures can destroy prestigious systems. Engineering institutions establish permanent reliability groups rather than treating each accident as isolated workmanship.</p>"},
      {id:"methods",title:"Methods",html:"<p>Supplier inspection, serialized component traceability, statistical process control, failure analysis, fatigue testing, vibration, environmental chambers, pressure cycles, maintenance schedules, and formal accident inquiries become standard practices.</p><p>Records connect a failed part to its batch, machine, inspector, service history, and design revision.</p>"},
      {id:"comet",title:"The Comet inquiries",html:"<p>British Comet structural failures lead to full-scale fatigue rigs, stronger manufacturer records, independent inquiry, and a new emphasis on repeated pressurization rather than static strength alone.</p><p>Britain's long-term export becomes confidence in its certification practice as much as any one airliner.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Space and nuclear requirements",html:"<p>Launch and reactor programs cannot rely on repair after failure. They require component qualification, redundant paths, controlled materials, signed test results, and scheduled replacement before visible breakdown.</p><p>The documentary burden becomes a major industrial cost and a barrier to entry.</p>"},
      {id:"civilian",title:"Civilian spillovers",html:"<p>Cars and appliances benefit through better heat treatment, seals, insulation, wiring, bearings, coatings, and maintenance information. The benefit is often a longer service life or fewer dangerous failures rather than a visible new feature.</p>"},
      {id:"labor",title:"Inspectors and technicians",html:"<p>Reliability creates new authority for inspectors, test engineers, maintenance planners, and certified technicians. Production workers increasingly record measurements and batch identity instead of merely completing a physical operation.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Certification and trust",html:"<p>States use certification to support exports and public confidence. German practice emphasizes signed plans and controlled suppliers. British practice emphasizes independent inquiry and service records. American practice emphasizes contractor qualification and liability. Japanese practice emphasizes production discipline and procedure.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Limits",html:"<p>Reliability records can be falsified, classification can obstruct inquiry, and certification can become protection for established firms. A compliant process cannot eliminate bad assumptions or political pressure.</p><p>The discipline reduces ordinary failure without making complex systems infallible.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"applied-systems-takeoff.html",kicker:"Historical setting",label:"Applied-Systems Takeoff"},{href:"technician-class.html",kicker:"Occupational consequence",label:"Certified Technician Class"},{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"High-reliability system",label:"Space Race"}],facts:[["Expansion","From the 1950s"],["Principal drivers","Aircraft, reactors, submarines, spaceflight"],["Core evidence","Serialized components and test records"],["Key tests","Fatigue, vibration, environment, pressure"],["British turning point","Comet structural inquiries"],["Civilian effect","Longer-lived components and safer products"],["Occupational beneficiaries","Inspectors and technicians"],["Principal limit","Certification cannot cure false assumptions"]]
  }),

  "civilian-cold-chain": materialArticle({
    slug:"civilian-cold-chain",title:"Civilian refrigeration and cold chain",eyebrow:"Food, medicine, and imperial logistics · 1947–1985",infoboxKicker:"Temperature-controlled distribution system",
    lead:"The civilian cold chain is the network of refrigeration equipment, warehouses, trucks, ships, shops, milk systems, pharmaceutical stores, and household refrigerators that keeps temperature-sensitive goods usable across distance. It is one of the most consequential but least theatrical technologies of the postwar order.",
    canon:"The importance of refrigerated storage, transport, pharmaceuticals, milk systems, household refrigeration, and their role in imperial geography is established. Precise adoption rates remain regional.",
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Postwar inheritance",html:"<p>Military food supply, field medicine, ship refrigeration, urban dairies, and industrial cold stores provide equipment and expertise after 1947. Wartime units are repaired and converted while firms build standardized compressors, insulation, controls, and service networks.</p>"},
      {id:"chain",title:"A system rather than an appliance",html:"<p>Cooling must persist from producer to warehouse, railcar or truck, port, ship, wholesaler, shop, and household. One failed compressor or power outage can destroy the value preserved by every earlier stage.</p><p>Thermometers, records, backup power, maintenance, and disciplined loading are therefore part of the technology.</p>"},
      {id:"food",title:"Food and urban supply",html:"<p>Milk, meat, fish, fruit, frozen goods, and prepared foods can travel farther and remain safer. Cities become less dependent on immediate seasonal hinterlands, while ports and producer regions gain access to distant markets.</p>"},
      {id:"medicine",title:"Pharmaceutical and medical use",html:"<p>Vaccines, blood products, laboratory reagents, and temperature-sensitive medicines require reliable storage and transport. Hospitals and clinics gain new treatments only where electricity, equipment, records, and repair can support them.</p>"},
      {id:"shipping",title:"Refrigerated ships and empire",html:"<p>Refrigerated ships and port stores reshape imperial geography by linking plantations, fisheries, settler farms, and metropolitan consumers. Cold infrastructure often follows concession ports and favored export corridors before serving local households.</p>"},
      {id:"home",title:"The household refrigerator",html:"<p>The refrigerator becomes a political symbol because it condenses power generation, chemistry, credit, distribution, housing wiring, and maintenance into one domestic object.</p><p>Ownership changes shopping frequency and food storage but remains sharply divided by class and region.</p>"},
      {id:"labor",title:"Technical labor",html:"<p>Refrigeration mechanics work across warehouses, ships, shops, farms, factories, and homes. Their gauges, refrigerants, electrical knowledge, and portable credentials make them an early form of the certified technician.</p>"},
      {id:"inequality",title:"Unequal access",html:"<p>A plantation or mine may possess modern export refrigeration while nearby residents lack reliable household power. Cold chains can reduce hunger and spoilage while also intensifying extraction by making distant commodity shipment profitable.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"technician-class.html",kicker:"Service occupation",label:"Certified Technician Class"},{href:"logistics-technical-standards.html",kicker:"Distribution setting",label:"Logistics and Technical Standards"},{href:"affluent-household-1985.html",kicker:"Domestic endpoint",label:"Affluent Household in 1985"}],facts:[["Development period","Accelerates after 1947"],["Core infrastructure","Warehouses, vehicles, ships, shops, homes"],["Principal food sectors","Milk, meat, fish, produce"],["Medical role","Vaccines, blood, reagents, medicines"],["Household emblem","Refrigerator-freezer"],["Key dependency","Reliable electricity and maintenance"],["Imperial effect","Long-distance commodity corridors"],["Central inequality","Export cooling before household access"]]
  }),

  "logistics-technical-standards": materialArticle({
    slug:"logistics-technical-standards",title:"Civilian logistics and technical standards",eyebrow:"Freight, interfaces, and bloc alignment · 1947–1985",infoboxKicker:"Commercial infrastructure system",
    lead:"Civilian logistics and technical standards are the pallets, crates, containers, rail dimensions, electrical systems, frequencies, documents, spare parts, measurements, and maintenance schools that make goods move and machines remain usable. They bind smaller states to blocs through daily operations more durably than diplomatic slogans.",
    canon:"The spread of pallets, forklifts, early containers, incompatible bloc fittings, and technical-assistance politics is established. Final container and several consumer-interface standards remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"handling",title:"Mechanized handling",html:"<p>Pallets, forklifts, standardized crates, warehouse racks, cranes, and documentation reduce manual handling, breakage, delay, and pilferage. Mechanization requires floors, loading bays, vehicle dimensions, and labor rules designed around the unit load.</p>"},
      {id:"containers",title:"Early freight containers",html:"<p>Road, rail, ship, and port operators experiment with reusable boxes during the 1950s and 1960s. Each bloc develops fittings, dimensions, documentation, and crane practices before a universal standard can emerge.</p><p>Exact mature bloc container names and dimensions remain unsettled.</p>"},
      {id:"documents",title:"Documents and records",html:"<p>A physical shipment travels with manifests, customs classifications, insurance, weights, seals, ownership claims, and routing instructions. Teleprinters and later computers make these records faster without eliminating paper.</p><p>Incompatible commodity and character codes can delay freight as effectively as an incompatible crane.</p>"},
      {id:"catalogue",title:"The parts catalogue",html:"<p>Vehicles, pumps, radios, appliances, and machine tools depend on numbered spares, service bulletins, drawings, gauges, and trained repairers. A country adopting one supplier's catalogue also adopts its measurements and replenishment channels.</p>"},
      {id:"assistance",title:"Technical assistance",html:"<p>Foreign programs provide equipment together with electrical conventions, rail practice, radio frequencies, maintenance schools, forms, and spare-parts contracts. This determines the operating language of infrastructure long after the original aid mission leaves.</p>"},
      {id:"bloc",title:"Standards as bloc power",html:"<p>A plug, tape format, film size, container fitting, frequency, or vehicle part can make switching suppliers expensive. Germany emphasizes certified continental systems. Japan emphasizes imperial procurement. Britain emphasizes Commonwealth interchange. America emphasizes market interfaces. The Latin bloc emphasizes negotiated interoperability.</p>"},
      {id:"gateways",title:"Gateways and conversion",html:"<p>Trade across standards requires adapters, repacking, transshipment, code conversion, dual-trained technicians, and liability agreements. Ports, Britain, and selected Latin institutions become valuable because they specialize in these gateways.</p>"},
      {id:"endpoint",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>Commercial détente increases cross-bloc freight without producing one technical world. Quotas, local assembly, protected finance, and incompatible standards preserve bloc supply chains.</p><p>Interoperability is extensive but deliberately mediated.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"software-portability.html",kicker:"Digital counterpart",label:"Software Portability"},{href:"protected-consumer-commerce.html",kicker:"Trade regime",label:"Protected Consumer Commerce"},{href:"surplus-motorization.html",kicker:"Earlier transport layer",label:"Surplus Motorization"}],facts:[["Early tools","Pallets, forklifts, standardized crates"],["Container convergence","Incomplete by 1985"],["Hidden infrastructure","Documents and parts catalogues"],["Political instrument","Technical assistance"],["Switching cost","Retraining, spares, conversion"],["Major gateway culture","British Commonwealth"],["1985 condition","Mediated interoperability"],["Open design area","Exact bloc container standards"]]
  }),

  "japanese-consumer-brands": materialArticle({
    slug:"japanese-consumer-brands",title:"Japanese consumer brands",eyebrow:"Asia-first corporate identity · 1947–1985",infoboxKicker:"Japanese commercial and design system",
    lead:"Japanese consumer brands develop for an Asia-first imperial market rather than treating the United States as their formative destination. Legal corporate names, imperial consumer marks, and local sales identities coexist across scripts, allowing products to signify Japanese modernity without disguising themselves as American goods.",
    canon:"The three-level identity model, Asia-first orientation, Totsuko continuity, Matsushita/National prominence, Honda, Datsun, Toyota, Prince, Sharp, and Canon treatments are established. Several companion marks and final product names remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"hierarchy",title:"Three levels of identity",html:"<ol><li>A legal corporate name identifies ownership and formal responsibility.</li><li>An imperial consumer mark travels across the Japanese system.</li><li>A local sales identity or description explains the product in the market language.</li></ol><p>The levels can appear together on packaging and service documents.</p>"},
      {id:"scripts",title:"Roman letters and companion marks",html:"<p>Roman letters travel across scripts but do not make a name English in meaning or political identity. China requires companion character marks and formal character clearance. Korea and Formosa receive more directly Japanese forms. Southeast Asian markets use romanized Japanese marks with local explanation.</p>"},
      {id:"electronics",title:"Totsuko, National, Sharp, and Canon",html:"<p><a href='tokyo-tsushin-kogyo.html'>Tokyo Tsushin Kogyo</a> retains its corporate name after 1958. Its durable consumer identity is Totsuko, potentially accompanied by a character mark. <a href='matsushita-electric-industrial.html'>Matsushita</a> and National remain more prominent than Panasonic.</p><p><a href='sharp-corporation.html'>Sharp</a> and <a href='canon-camera.html'>Canon</a> survive as viable international marks in electronics, appliances, office equipment, and optics.</p>"},
      {id:"mobility",title:"Honda, Nissan–Datsun, Toyota, and Prince",html:"<p>The <a href='japanese-motor-industry.html'>Japanese motor industry</a> serves an Asia-first market. <a href='honda-motor-company.html'>Honda</a> keeps its name. Taiwan and Southeast Asia shape its formative overseas market. <a href='nissan-motor-company.html'>Nissan</a> remains the parent group and Datsun the mass export marque.</p><p><a href='toyota-motor-company.html'>Toyota</a> retains Crown and Century. No Lexus mark is created during the reference period. <a href='prince-motor-company.html'>Prince</a> may remain an independent prestige and performance competitor.</p>"},
      {id:"meaning",title:"Japanese modernity",html:"<p>Compact radios, cameras, motorcycles, calculators, watches, cassette players, televisions, and later video and games are marketed as precise products of Japanese order and technical skill.</p><p>Association with Japan is an advantage inside much of the sphere, not a provenance to conceal.</p>"},
      {id:"precision",title:"Optics, timing, and control marks",html:"<p><a href='nippon-kogaku.html'>Nippon Kogaku</a> carries Nikon cameras and Nikkor lenses into professional and consumer markets, while <a href='minolta-camera.html'>Minolta</a> competes in cameras, sensing, and optical equipment. <a href='seiko.html'>Seiko</a> connects watches and clocks to quartz timing, and <a href='casio-computer.html'>Casio</a> approaches portable electronics through calculators.</p><p><a href='omron-tateisi-electric.html'>Tateisi Electric</a> uses the OMRON mark for sensing and control. <a href='yaskawa-electric.html'>Yaskawa</a> is identified with motors, servo systems, and mechatronics.</p>"},
      {id:"systems",title:"Electrical and systems houses",html:"<p><a href='hitachi.html'>Hitachi</a> and <a href='toshiba.html'>Toshiba</a> span heavy electrical equipment, infrastructure, appliances, electronics, and computing. <a href='nippon-electric-company.html'>Nippon Electric Company</a> and <a href='fujitsu.html'>Fujitsu</a> connect telecommunications to computers, semiconductors, and orbital ground systems.</p><p><a href='mitsubishi-electric.html'>Mitsubishi Electric</a> joins power and automation to strategic electronics. <a href='nakajima-industrial-company.html'>Nakajima Industrial Company</a> remains the protected aviation-machinery concern formed from Nakajima Aircraft.</p>"},
      {id:"localization",title:"Localization and service",html:"<p>Manuals, labels, warranties, dealer signs, and repair networks use local languages where practical, but strategic models and parts systems remain centrally controlled. Localization expands usability without necessarily transferring ownership or advanced production.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Corporate hierarchy and sovereignty",html:"<p>Associated-state merchants and manufacturers may distribute or assemble Japanese products under local identities. Zaibatsu finance and intellectual property keep high-value control in Japan even where the visible storefront appears national.</p>"},
      {id:"endpoint",title:"Position in 1985",html:"<p>Japanese products are prominent in electronics, optics, motorcycles, watches, video, games, and factory controls across Asia and increasingly beyond it. Commercial détente broadens their Western presence without forcing convergence on American names.</p><p>Several exact export marks remain intentionally unsettled.</p>"}
    ],related:[{href:"japanese-motor-industry.html",kicker:"Mobility sector",label:"Motor Industry of Japan"},{href:"tokyo-tsushin-kogyo.html",kicker:"Electronics firm",label:"Tokyo Tsushin Kogyo"},{href:"honda-motor-company.html",kicker:"Mobility firm",label:"Honda Motor Company"},{href:"matsushita-electric-industrial.html",kicker:"Mass electrical firm",label:"Matsushita Electric Industrial"},{href:"nippon-kogaku.html",kicker:"Optical firm",label:"Nippon Kogaku"},{href:"seiko.html",kicker:"Timing firm",label:"Seiko"},{href:"casio-computer.html",kicker:"Calculator firm",label:"Casio Computer"},{href:"omron-tateisi-electric.html",kicker:"Control firm",label:"Tateisi Electric"},{href:"yaskawa-electric.html",kicker:"Automation firm",label:"Yaskawa Electric"},{href:"japanese-yen-area.html",kicker:"Commercial system",label:"Japanese Yen Area"},{href:"protected-consumer-commerce.html",kicker:"Cross-bloc market",label:"Protected Consumer Commerce"}],facts:[["Market orientation","Asia first"],["Identity levels","Corporate, imperial mark, local sales identity"],["Tokyo Tsushin mark","Totsuko"],["Matsushita mark","National remains prominent"],["Nissan export marque","Datsun"],["Toyota luxury names","Crown and Century"],["Postwar formative markets","Taiwan and Southeast Asia"],["Open area","Several companion and format marks"]]
  })
});
